## Migration Analysis

Your code has **3 patterns** that break on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 | Pattern |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Cancellable work loop |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable | Blocking call interruption |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Works, but can't be cancelled cooperatively | Blocking call |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            // GetConsumingEnumerable blocks until an item is available
            // and throws OperationCanceledException when the token is cancelled,
            // replacing both ManualResetEvent and Thread.Interrupt in one call.
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do.
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();              // wakes the blocked thread cooperatively
        _queue.CompleteAdding();     // signals no more items
        _thread?.Join(5000);        // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback needed — the token guarantees the loop exits.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

## What changed and why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `BlockingCollection<string>` | Thread-safe producer/consumer queue with built-in blocking — eliminates manual signaling and locking |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` | Cooperatively wakes `GetConsumingEnumerable` via its token |
| `Thread.Abort()` (fallback) | Removed entirely | Not needed — `Cancel()` guarantees the loop exits |
| No `IDisposable` | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` and `BlockingCollection` must be disposed to avoid timer/handle leaks |

## Key Pitfalls

1. **Race condition in original code** — Multiple `Enqueue` calls could queue N items but `WaitOne`/`Reset` only processes one before re-blocking. `BlockingCollection` fixes this naturally.

2. **Don't forget `IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks internal timers/callbacks if not disposed. Always wrap or implement `IDisposable`.

3. **`Thread.Abort` has no equivalent** — There is no way to forcibly kill a managed thread in .NET 8. If `Handle(item)` can hang indefinitely, add a cancellation token to it too:
   ```csharp
   private void Handle(string item, CancellationToken token)
   {
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
       // ... your logic, checking token at safe points
   }
   ```

4. **Don't catch and swallow `OperationCanceledException`** deep in the call chain — only catch it at the top-level loop. Let it propagate from inner methods.

5. **`CompleteAdding()` order matters** — Call `Cancel()` *before* `CompleteAdding()` so the blocked `GetConsumingEnumerable` wakes immediately rather than waiting to drain remaining items.
